{
  "slug": "student-loan-forgiveness-programs",
  "metaTitle": "Student Loan Forgiveness Programs and Who Qualifies",
  "metaDescription": "See which student loan forgiveness programs exist, the test for each one, and why private loans and the Tiered Standard plan offer no forgiveness.",
  "h1": "Student Loan Forgiveness Programs by Qualifying Test",
  "cardBlurb": "Five federal programs can forgive or discharge student loans, and each one has its own test for who qualifies.",
  "introText": "Student loan forgiveness programs come in five federal routes, and each has its own test: Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF), Teacher Loan Forgiveness, income-driven plan forgiveness, total and permanent disability discharge, and borrower defense for school misconduct. At ModernWallet, we write plain-language money guides, and the student loan mistake we see most often is a borrower waiting for one blanket program that does not exist.\n\nEach test looks at something different. PSLF counts 120 payments made while you work in public service, and Teacher Loan Forgiveness needs five straight school years at a low-income school. Income-driven forgiveness comes after a long repayment period, and a disability discharge depends on medical or benefits paperwork.\n\nEvery program here covers federal loans only. If you hold private student loans, none of these programs applies to them, and [types of student loans](/guides/types-of-student-loans/) shows how to tell which kind you have.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Federal Student Loan Forgiveness Programs and Who Qualifies",
      "body": "Five federal programs can forgive or discharge a student loan, and each has its own qualifying test.\n\n- **Public Service Loan Forgiveness:** you make 120 monthly payments on an eligible Direct Loan after October 1, 2007, under a qualifying repayment plan, while you work in a public service job for each of those payments.\n- **Teacher Loan Forgiveness:** you teach full time for five consecutive complete school years at a school that meets the low-income criteria, and your loan is not in default. The cap is $5,000, or $17,500 for qualifying secondary school math or science teachers and qualifying special education teachers.\n- **Income-driven plan forgiveness:** you repay on an income-driven plan for an extended period. Federal law caps Income-Contingent Repayment (ICR) at 25 years, and other plans set their own periods.\n- **Total and permanent disability discharge:** your Direct Loan is discharged if you become totally and permanently disabled, backed by a medical certification, Social Security disability benefits records, or a Department of Veterans Affairs unemployability finding.\n- **Borrower defense:** if your school misled or defrauded you, you can apply for relief through [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/).\n\nNo blanket program sits on this list. If you work full time in government or emergency management, start with PSLF, and add Teacher Loan Forgiveness to your check if you teach at a low-income school.\n\nIf you meet none of these tests, the choice left to you is a repayment plan. [The student loan Standard Repayment Plan changes](/guides/student-loan-standard-repayment-plan/) explains the plans that replaced the old menu."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Public Service Loan Forgiveness",
      "body": "Public Service Loan Forgiveness forgives the remaining balance on an eligible Direct Loan after 120 monthly payments made while you work in public service. The rules sit in [20 U.S.C. 1087e(m)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/20/1087e), part of the United States Code (U.S.C.), and they set three conditions.\n\n- You make 120 monthly payments on an eligible Federal Direct Loan after October 1, 2007.\n- Each payment runs under a qualifying repayment plan.\n- You hold a public service job during the period you make each of those 120 payments.\n\nThe statute defines a public service job to include a full-time job in emergency management or government, excluding time as a member of Congress, plus other fields it lists. The statute text does not name individual employers, so confirm yours on [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/) before you count on PSLF. The statute text also does not say the 120 payments must be consecutive.\n\nOnly four loan types qualify: Federal Direct Stafford, Direct PLUS, Direct Unsubsidized Stafford, and Direct Consolidation loans. Refinancing federal loans with a private lender ends PSLF eligibility, so read [should you refinance student loans](/guides/should-you-refinance-student-loans/) before you sign a refinance offer.\n\nOur answer for a government worker flips in three cases: your job falls outside the statute's public service definition, your payments ran under a plan that does not qualify, or your loans are not Direct Loans. In any of those cases, look next at income-driven forgiveness."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Income-Driven Plan Forgiveness and the 2026 Plan Changes",
      "body": "Income-driven plan forgiveness cancels the balance left after an extended repayment period, and the length of that period depends on the plan and the borrower. For Income-Contingent Repayment, [20 U.S.C. 1087e(d)(1)(D)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/20/1087e) says repayment is \"paid over an extended period of time prescribed by the Secretary, not to exceed 25 years.\"\n\nThe same statute makes ICR available \"before June 30, 2028.\" ICR is not available for Direct PLUS loans made on behalf of a dependent student, so a parent who borrowed that way cannot use ICR for that loan. Pay As You Earn (PAYE) and the other income-driven plans each set their own forgiveness period, which you can confirm for your loans on [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/).\n\nThe plan menu changed on July 1, 2026. The One Big Beautiful Bill Act began replacing the old repayment plans with two options:\n\n- A Tiered Standard plan, which offers no forgiveness at the end of its term.\n- An income-driven Repayment Assistance Plan (RAP).\n\nBorrowers on ICR or PAYE face a full sunset date of July 1, 2028 under the law. We do not have a confirmed RAP forgiveness period, so look up RAP's current terms on [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/) before you move to it.\n\nIf income-driven forgiveness is your goal, the Tiered Standard plan cannot get you there. [The student loan Standard Repayment Plan changes](/guides/student-loan-standard-repayment-plan/) covers how the new plans work."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Teacher Loan Forgiveness",
      "body": "Teacher Loan Forgiveness repays up to $5,000 for a full-time teacher who finishes five consecutive complete school years at a low-income school. The limit rises to $17,500 for qualifying secondary school math or science teachers and qualifying special education teachers.\n\n[20 U.S.C. 1078-10](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/20/1078-10) sets three tests:\n\n- Full-time employment as a teacher for 5 consecutive complete school years.\n- A school that meets the low-income criteria.\n- A loan that is not in default.\n\nThe $5,000 figure is an aggregate cap, so it is the most the Secretary of Education may repay in total under the standard limit. A teacher who qualifies for the higher limit can get up to $12,500 more ($17,500 minus $5,000).\n\nA loan in default does not qualify. If you have fallen behind, [what happens if you don't pay your student loans](/guides/what-happens-if-you-dont-pay-your-student-loans/) covers what default leads to, and you can run your paycheck numbers in the [student loan wage garnishment calculator](/student-loan-wage-garnishment-calculator/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Disability Discharge and Borrower Defense",
      "body": "A Direct Loan is discharged when the borrower becomes totally and permanently disabled, under [34 CFR 685.213](https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/34/685.213) in the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR). The regulation accepts three kinds of proof:\n\n- A certification from a physician (a doctor of medicine or osteopathy legally authorized to practice in a state), a nurse practitioner, a physician assistant, or a certified psychologist.\n- Social Security Administration documentation showing you receive Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) or Supplemental Security Income (SSI) disability benefits.\n- For veterans, a Department of Veterans Affairs determination that you are unemployable due to a service-connected disability.\n\nThe discharge can be reversed. Your loan can be reinstated if, within 3 years after the discharge, you receive a new TEACH Grant (Teacher Education Assistance for College and Higher Education Grant) or a new Direct Loan, with exceptions for certain consolidation loans.\n\nThe regulation sets no ongoing monitoring period after discharge. That makes the 3-year window on new federal aid the rule to plan around if you are thinking about going back to school.\n\nBorrower defense is for borrowers whose school misled or defrauded them. You apply on [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/), which is also where to check how claims are currently being decided, since we could not confirm that ourselves."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Taxes on Forgiven Student Loans",
      "body": "Federal income tax on forgiven student debt depends on the kind of discharge and the year it happens. Three rules apply, based on [26 U.S.C. 108](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/108):\n\n- Discharges for death or total and permanent disability after December 31, 2025 are excluded from federal gross income under section 108(f)(5).\n- A temporary exclusion covered certain student loan discharges after December 31, 2020 and before January 1, 2026.\n- Forgiveness not covered by an exclusion is generally income in the year it is canceled.\n\nIf your forgiveness falls under that third rule, plan for it to count as income in the year of cancellation. [Is student loan forgiveness taxable](/guides/is-student-loan-forgiveness-taxable/) walks through each exclusion in more detail."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to Check Which Student Loan Forgiveness Programs Fit You",
      "body": "You can match your loans to a program in four steps, starting with your loan type on studentaid.gov.\n\n1. **Confirm your loan type.** Log in at [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/) and list each loan. PSLF counts only Direct Loans, and [types of student loans](/guides/types-of-student-loans/) explains how the categories differ.\n2. **Confirm your job.** PSLF needs a public service job for every counted payment. Teacher Loan Forgiveness needs five consecutive complete school years of full-time teaching at a low-income school.\n3. **Confirm your repayment plan.** PSLF payments must run under a qualifying plan. ICR and PAYE face a July 1, 2028 sunset, and the Tiered Standard plan ends with no forgiveness.\n4. **Keep records.** Save every payment record and proof of employment for each period, because PSLF ties each of the 120 payments to the job you held when you made it.\n\nPrivate student loans are not covered by any of these federal programs. If all of your debt is private, none of these routes applies to it.\n\nLog in to [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/) today, pull your loan list, and match each loan against the student loan forgiveness programs above."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Who qualifies for student loan forgiveness now?",
      "answer": "You qualify if you meet the test for one specific federal program. That means 120 qualifying payments in public service for Public Service Loan Forgiveness, five consecutive complete school years at a low-income school for Teacher Loan Forgiveness, a long income-driven repayment period, total and permanent disability, or school misconduct for borrower defense. There is no blanket program, and private loans are excluded."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is student loan forgiveness still available?",
      "answer": "Yes. Federal statute and regulation text sets out the terms for Public Service Loan Forgiveness, Teacher Loan Forgiveness, Income-Contingent Repayment, and total and permanent disability discharge. ICR and PAYE face a sunset on July 1, 2028, and you can check borrower defense status on [studentaid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/)."
